{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' SYMBOLS.md && sed -n '1,220p' ERRORS.md && sed -n '1,260p' CONFIGS.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Dynamic Symbol Surface\n\nGenerated from:\n\n```text\nnm -D --defined-only ../c_src/build/libharvest-work-ACXBgi.so\nnm -D --defined-only target/release/libnormalize_lib.so\n```\n\n| C symbol | C type | Rust symbol | Rust type | Status |\n|----------|--------|-------------|-----------|--------|\n| `normalize` | `T` | `normalize` | `T` | present |\n\nThe C library has one globally defined dynamic symbol. Its other dynamic\nentries are undefined runtime imports (`memset`, `sqrtf`) or weak toolchain\nsymbols, not public symbols implemented by this library.\n\nMissing C-defined symbols in Rust: **0**.\n# Error Surface\n\nMechanical audit covered `c_src/include/lib.h` and `c_src/src/lib.c` for:\nerror-return macros/statements, error enums, `assert`, explicit range checks,\nnull checks, and min/max constants.\n\n| # | function | trigger (the exact invalid input/condition) | expected C result | tested |\n|---|----------|---------------------------------------------|-------------------|--------|\n\nThere are **no explicit rejection or error paths** in the C source. In\nparticular, `normalize` returns `void` and performs no null or range checks.\nCrash-prone generic FFI boundaries (null pointers and negative/oversized\nlengths) are therefore tested as process outcomes rather than invented as\nerror returns.\n# Configuration Surface\n\nThe sole public entry point is the low-level API `normalize(float *dest,\nconst float *src, int size)`. There are no runtime options, modes, flags,\nenums, compile-time features, convenience wrappers, or binaries.\n\nThe rows below are the cross-product pruned to distinctions made by the C\nloops and branches: `size <= 0` versus positive one/many, `sum > 0` versus\nfalse, finite versus infinite positive sums, and exact/non-exact pointer\naliasing. Partial overlap is split by direction because the positive-sum\nwrite loop proceeds forward and can overwrite unread source elements.\n\n| # | entry point(s) | configuration (options set + input shape) | [ ] |\n|---|----------------|--------------------------------------------|-----|\n| 1 | `normalize` | `size == 0`; distinct valid `dest` and `src`; no-op `memset(..., 0)` path | [ ] |\n| 2 | `normalize` | `size == 0`; exact in-place alias (`dest == src`); no-op alias path | [ ] |\n| 3 | `normalize` | `size == 1`; finite positive sum; disjoint buffers | [ ] |\n| 4 | `normalize` | `size == 1`; finite positive sum; exact in-place alias | [ ] |\n| 5 | `normalize` | `size > 1`; finite positive sum; disjoint buffers | [ ] |\n| 6 | `normalize` | `size > 1`; finite positive sum; exact in-place alias | [ ] |\n| 7 | `normalize` | `size > 1`; finite positive sum; partial overlap with `dest < src` | [ ] |\n| 8 | `normalize` | `size > 1`; finite positive sum; partial overlap with `dest > src` (forward writes can alter unread source) | [ ] |\n| 9 | `normalize` | `size == 1`; zero sum; disjoint buffers are zero-filled | [ ] |\n| 10 | `normalize` | `size == 1`; zero sum; exact in-place alias is left byte-identical | [ ] |\n| 11 | `normalize` | `size > 1`; zero sum; disjoint buffers are zero-filled | [ ] |\n| 12 | `normalize` | `size > 1`; zero sum; exact in-place alias is left byte-identical | [ ] |\n| 13 | `normalize` | `size > 1`; zero sum; partial overlap with `dest < src` is zero-filled | [ ] |\n| 14 | `normalize` | `size > 1`; zero sum; partial overlap with `dest > src` is zero-filled | [ ] |\n| 15 | `normalize` | positive size; sum becomes NaN; disjoint buffers are zero-filled | [ ] |\n| 16 | `normalize` | positive size; sum becomes NaN; exact in-place alias is left byte-identical | [ ] |\n| 17 | `normalize` | positive size; sum becomes NaN; partially overlapping buffers are zero-filled at `dest` | [ ] |\n| 18 | `normalize` | positive size; sum is positive infinity (infinite input or finite overflow); disjoint buffers | [ ] |\n| 19 | `normalize` | positive size; sum is positive infinity; exact in-place alias | [ ] |\n| 20 | `normalize` | `size > 1`; sum is positive infinity; partial overlap with `dest < src` | [ ] |\n| 21 | `normalize` | `size > 1`; sum is positive infinity; partial overlap with `dest > src` | [ ] |\n| 22 | `normalize` | positive nonzero subnormal inputs whose squares underflow, yielding zero sum; disjoint buffers | [ ] |\n| 23 | `normalize` | positive nonzero subnormal inputs whose squares underflow, yielding zero sum; exact in-place alias | [ ] |\n| 24 | `normalize` | `size < 0`; exact alias (including equal null pointers); loops skipped and no `memset` | [ ] |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
